Chapter: Fat16Overwrite
  cites Foreword chapter Fat16

 Writing a file twice replaces it.

 It used to commit a SECOND directory entry under the same name.
 fat16-find-free-slot answers the first free slot and knows nothing about
 names, so the second write claimed a fresh cluster chain and wrote a
 second entry; readers take whichever they meet first, which is the older
 one sitting in the lower slot. The write answered True, the clusters
 were really spent, and the file still read back its previous contents.
 fat16-create-directory has guarded against exactly this since it was
 written; files never got the same guard.

 The read-back after the second write is the whole test, and the reason
 is that the obvious assertion is the one that cannot fail. A test that
 wrote twice and checked the write ANSWERED True passed before this fix
 and after it. A test that checked the file still held its first contents
 passed before the fix for the bug's reason and after it for no reason at
 all. Only demanding the NEW contents separates them, and before the fix
 this line read "first".

 The root and a subdirectory are both exercised because they reach the
 disk through different slot finders -- fat16-find-free-slot for the root
 and fat16-find-or-grow-slot-in-dir for a chain -- and the defect was in
 both. Fixing one and shipping it as fixed is the shape of failure this
 battery is supposed to make hard.

 The listing counts are the leak half, and they are what the read-back
 alone cannot see. A replacement that wrote the new bytes but still added
 an entry would return the right contents from the newer entry and grow
 the directory every time it was written. One name written three times
 must leave exactly one entry behind it.

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console, Device.Block] Nothing
  opening = act
    vol <- fat16-init 2048

    a <- fat16-write-file "OW.TXT" "first"
    b <- fat16-write-file "OW.TXT" "second"
    print-line-uni ("writes " & show a & " " & show b)

    back <- fat16-read-text vol "OW.TXT"
    print-line-uni ("root reads " & ow-text back)

    c <- fat16-write-file "OW.TXT" "third"
    names <- fat16-list-root vol
    print-line-uni ("root entries " & show (list-length names))

    d <- fat16-create-directory vol "SUB"
    e <- fat16-write-file "SUB/IN.TXT" "one"
    f <- fat16-write-file "SUB/IN.TXT" "two"
    print-line-uni ("subdir writes " & show e & " " & show f)

    deep <- fat16-read-text vol "SUB/IN.TXT"
    print-line-uni ("subdir reads " & ow-text deep)
  end

Section: Reporting

  ow-text : Maybe Text -> Text
  ow-text (m) = when m
    is Just (t) -> t
    is None -> "<none>"
